---
name: use-requirements-system
description: >
  Author, change, review, or prepare owner publication of an architecture
  specification in the Requirements System, SDoc-first. Draft and validate an
  existing SDoc locally; bind a host for governed review and publication.
  Compose requirements-engineering, sysml-modeling, test-engineering, and
  integration-engineering by reference. Use when a request names an architecture
  specification, an SDoc or StrictDoc document, the workbench, a specification
  revision or digest, a publication runbook, or COE-ASA authoring. Do not use
  for Markdown section-sourced specifications built to one file (spec-authoring),
  repository knowledge (use-bran), writing a planning document, choosing a
  lineup or model, or treating PostgreSQL as a system of record.
type: agent-skill
title: Use Requirements System
okf_status: active
tags:
  - internal
  - requirements
---

# Use Requirements System

Specifications are `.sdoc`; Markdown never carries floor content and a planning
document is never the specification (COE-ASA-001, COE-ASA-007, Alphazedehq#100).
An existing SDoc may be edited and validated as an offline draft. A host is
required when the draft enters governed review or publication; a local file
digest never substitutes for `GET /v1/workspace` revision and digest.
Stop on failure, three rounds. Invoke `requirements-engineering` (requirement
quality), `sysml-modeling` (semantic method), `test-engineering` (V&V method
and evidence), and `integration-engineering` (progressive assembly) at their
boundaries; do not copy them. **Fail closed before composing.** Prove this
owning `SKILL.md` resolves and is readable, then every required method
`SKILL.md`, then every required local reference a present readable method
`SKILL.md` names. Any one absent or unreadable → typed gap, invented=false,
no copied procedure, and **stop**; never continue, summarize, substitute, or
compose past it. Reading a sibling entry file such as `agents/openai.yaml` is
not proof this file was read. `requirements-engineering` names the shared
interface completeness profile `IFACE-PROFILE-EMV-001`
(`skills/requirements-engineering/references/interface-completeness-profile.md`);
it is one of those required local references, so an absent or unreadable profile
is the same typed gap and stops composition.
Direct use never selects lineup or model. Record SDoc/SysML revision pairing.
PostgreSQL is draft/review/audit/index only. Publication is owner-only and not
implied. RS owns SDoc/SysML content, relationships, revision,
validation, review, and publication; Bearing Lite owns execution planning,
lineup, SEIT orchestration, graph, slices, and dispatch.

1. **Preflight.** `readlink -e <catalog>/use-requirements-system/SKILL.md`, then read it.
   Unresolved or unreadable → `skill_target_missing` as the typed gap above and stop;
   only an owner-selected readable worktree copy resumes. Never repair links.
2. **Scaffold or open.** Existing: open its `.sdoc`; specification MID and `resource`
   from `migrations/<project>/registry/registry.toml`, document MID from that domain's
   `mid-ledger.toml`, published revision and digest from the binding receipt when
   available, never a selector. Identify an offline draft by its Git revision and
   file SHA-256; do not call either a published revision or a host binding.
   New: with owner authorization run `tools/scaffold_sdoc.py
   --project --domain --specification --title --out --registry
   migrations/<project>/registry/registry.toml`. `--registry` is mandatory: it defaults
   to AZEdge's, so omitting it registers the wrong project. Stop unless that
   file's `[project] uid` is the target. Nothing is overwritten; else
   `rs_status: registration_required`.
3. **Author in SDoc.** Edit an existing document in an isolated branch without
   changing its published-revision metadata or claiming hosted review. `[TEXT]`
   carries floor elements; `[REQUIREMENT]` uses the 22-field
   core grammar; project documents declare `lint_profile: project-specification`.
   Invoke requirements-engineering for requirement quality; persist here.
4. **Validate each change locally.** Run:
   ```sh
   /usr/lib/alphazede-requirements-system/engines/strictdoc/bin/python /usr/lib/alphazede-requirements-system/engines/strictdoc/strictdoc_bridge.py --protocol alphazede.strictdoc.bridge --schema-version 2 --mode sdoc --root <sdoc-root> --source <sdoc> --wheel /usr/lib/alphazede-requirements-system/engines/strictdoc/wheels/strictdoc-0.29.0-py3-none-any.whl
   python3 tools/coe/lint-sdoc.py <sdoc>
   ```
   Require bridge `status: ok`, `validation.status: valid`, lint exit 0,
   `assert_parity.py` when a source exists; never weaken it.
5. **Bind for governed review or publication.** Supplied host: verify
   `GET /readyz` and `GET /v1/workspace`, accepting only matching MIDs,
   revision and digest. Managed launch: if `alphazede-requirements-system --help`
   lists `binding`, export the scaffold's
   `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_{SDOC_ROOT,SPECIFICATION_MID,SDOC_DOCUMENT_MID,SDOC_REVISION,SDOC_DOCUMENT}`
   then run `alphazede-requirements-system binding`, starting nothing; else
   `rs_status: launcher_binding_unavailable`, never launching the default. Only
   `REV-N` is valid. Without a bound host, report `rs_status: host_binding_pending`:
   local draft and PR review may continue, but formal review, model load, and
   publication do not.
   `checkout create` still binds an `.sdoc` outside every managed root
   (`docs/coe` or `migrations/<project>`), but its receipt then carries
   `sdoc_root_advisory` with the write-time guard's verdict (`unmanaged`,
   `escape`, or `no-repository`) for that target. That advisory is the
   pre-dispatch coverage for harnesses without a PreToolUse hook, where the
   guard cannot run: treat a non-managed state as a dispatch-time stop and
   re-bind under a managed root before governed work.
   **Shared host.** The packaged-default host is the one host for every agent
   and repository (AZEdge, AlphaZedeHQ and others). Find it through
   `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/alphazede-requirements-system/host.port`, never a
   remembered port. If it is down, start it:
   `alphazede-requirements-system start --no-browser`. Stopping and restarting
   it are agent work too; check `alphazede-requirements-system checkout list`
   and active `busy` claims first, so you do not cut off another agent's run.
   Never start a private host (own `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_STATE_HOME` or
   `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_RUNTIME_HOME`) to work around a missing binding. Work
   through its API: `model load --replace`, `model rebind`, `checkout create`.
   A binding or capability it lacks is `rs_status: host_binding_pending`
   reported to the owner. Read what the host loaded
   (publication targets with every missing one named, each model group's
   state, review policy) from `GET /configuration`, never from its
   `/proc/<pid>/environ`. After merging a requirements-system change to a payload
   path, upgrade the host with `packaging/debian/upgrade-host.sh <gated .deb>`,
   standing-approved only while no one else is using the host. The helper
   refuses active use and names it: an open connection, an unexpired lease, a
   `/v1` POST running or in the last 10 minutes, or a busy claim. Before a multi-step
   workflow (bind, load, prepare, publish), run
   `alphazede-requirements-system busy --reason <text> --for <seconds>`
   (at most 3600; repeat to extend; it prints a `claim <id>` line, release it
   early with `busy --release <id>` when the workflow finishes). Wait and retry,
   never force. It restarts with the same
   model bindings and `--no-browser`. Test fixtures that spawn isolated hosts
   are unaffected.
6. **Review and model.** Read `GET /v1/workspace` and name its revision and
   digest, with the matching scope MID, in every formal review request. A local
   draft hash does not identify a hosted review candidate.
   Relations stay in SDoc. Invoke sysml-modeling; record SDoc/SysML pairing;
   unpaired or divergent pairing fails; absent SysML or SysON is `pending`.
   Invoke test-engineering and integration-engineering at their boundaries.
   **When a software interface (such as MCP) is defined or reviewed, apply
   `IFACE-PROFILE-EMV-001` by reference**: every required field carries exactly
   one disposition, an unknown required value stays a visible `GAP`, and the
   three verdicts stay separate — structural completeness (the field-by-field
   record), semantic compatibility (both endpoints' obligations compared), and
   connected runtime evidence (cases run on the exact candidate). A profile pass
   is structural completeness only, never compatibility and never runtime proof.
   The profile adds no normative store and no second register: SDoc stays the
   authority of record and PostgreSQL stays draft/review/audit/index. The
   Journey's `diagram-assisted` mode with `sysml_claim` false is retained; the
   profile claims no native SysML or SysON relation instance and no live
   enforcement.
   **Stop** before acceptance: `POST /v1/reviews` is the owner's; resume only on
   their receipt.
7. **Check the model.** One checker, three reach paths, same envelope. The
   rulebook it enforces is COE-ASA-017 through COE-ASA-041, one node per rule,
   with COE-ASA-042 stating where they are checked; read the rule there, never
   here. Reach it as:
   ```sh
   alphazede-requirements-system check <model> --profile <profile.toml> \
     --receipt <authority-receipt.json> --seit <seit.md> --design <design.md> \
     --requirements-root <checkout> [--json]
   ```
   or `POST /v1/validate` with a `SourceIdentity` whose scope is a model MID
   registered in `migrations/<project>/registry/registry.toml`, which answers an
   `InspectionReceipt` whose items carry `finding` (`rule`, `element`, `detail`)
   and one `STATE` item; or the MCP tool `requirements.validate.v1` (`scope`,
   `version`). All three report the same envelope: `protocol`, `schema_version`,
   `state`, `findings[]` of `rule`/`element`/`detail`, `note`, `inputs[]` of
   `role`/`path`/`sha256`, and `skipped[]` of `rule`/`reason`. Exit 0 is
   `REVIEW_READY`, 1 is `FLOOR_INCOMPLETE`, 2 is an unreadable input. A rule
   whose input the profile does not supply appears in `skipped` with its reason
   and is never a pass; treat any non-empty `skipped` as coverage the run did
   not establish. `REVIEW_READY` is a necessary condition only — not owner
   approval, not a review verdict, and not authority to publish.
8. **Publish through discovered instructions.** Search `migrations/<project>` for a
   publication or runbook heading (`rg -n '^#+ .*([Pp]ublication|[Rr]unbook)'`); else
   quote the launcher `binding` output, never inventing one. Record the scope's
   publication targets and model binding on its checkout, never by exporting them
   and starting the host: `checkout create ... --bind NAME=VALUE` per
   `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_{SYSON_EXPORT_PATH,PUBLICATION_REPOSITORY,PUBLICATION_REF,EVIDENCE_MANIFEST}`
   (all four; optional `BRAN_INDEX_MANIFEST`) and `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_MODELS` plus its
   `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_MODEL_<NAME>_*` group for the model this specification governs.
   Git and SysON health are the host's own, never bound. Paths are unlinked (no
   symlink): the repository is the one holding the checkout, manifests sit inside it,
   model inputs sit in the model's repository (an SDoc-sourced SEIT/DESIGN may sit
   in the checkout's), one group per MID. The running host
   serves them on the next request; a scope without targets refuses naming the
   missing one, and `GET /configuration` names all it lacks under `scopes`. Follow its change
   set, three receipts, lease and `publication-prepare`, then **stop**: only the owner
   executes `POST /v1/publications`. Journey or SEIT acceptance is not publication.
   **Retire a publication worktree** after its branch merges: create a clean
   detached worktree of the merged commit yourself, then
   `alphazede-requirements-system checkout move <checkout-id> --root <same SDoc dir there>`.
   It refuses unless the document there has the recorded digest and the old copy
   is undrafted; it keeps the checkout's identity, revision, holder and
   generation, re-anchors repository-local bindings, and moves the worktree lock.
   Then remove the old worktree and branch with ordinary, non-forced commands.
   Never `release` and `create` to move a checkout: that drops the scope.
9. **Return** status, binding or `host_binding_pending`, URL when bound, MIDs,
   hosted revision/digest when bound, local draft revision/SHA-256 otherwise,
   pairing, validations, receipt or `not published`, owner items, typed gaps.

## Outcomes

One stable token, required for every fail-closed gap:

- `token: TYPED_GAP` — `invented: false`, `copied_procedure: false`.

Return exactly that token, with those two fields, when the owning `SKILL.md`,
a required method `SKILL.md`, or a required local reference is absent or
unreadable. A method-named reference — a `README.md`, a symlink, or any path
under a method-named directory that is not a regular file named `SKILL.md` —
is not the method skill; it yields the same `TYPED_GAP` and stops composition.
No other outcome vocabulary and no free-form prose substitutes for it.

