# Agent Working Agreement

The agent skill and the write-time guard for this system live here, in
`skills/use-requirements-system/` and `hooks/`; see the README section on them.

For Debian packaging, follow the authoritative [build contract](packaging/debian/BUILDING.md).
A probe or harness names the build it bound and stops any engine it started before deleting that engine's root.

## Architecture decisions

MVP work affecting native SDoc/SysON identity, artifact scope, or leases must
follow [ADR-0001](docs/adr/0001-native-identity-and-artifact-scope.md) until a
published Requirements System SDoc supersedes it.

The Requirements System host is a local dev/test host; running it is agent work.
Every agent uses the packaged-default host. Find it through
`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/alphazede-requirements-system/host.port`. If it is down, start it:
`alphazede-requirements-system start --no-browser`. Stopping and restarting it are
agent work too. Check `alphazede-requirements-system checkout list` and active `busy`
claims first, so you do not cut off another agent's run. Never run a private host
with own `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_STATE_HOME` or `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_RUNTIME_HOME`. After merging
a requirements-system change to a payload path, upgrade the host with
`packaging/debian/upgrade-host.sh <gated .deb>`; never force an upgrade while the
host is in use.

Keep work within the approved slice, exact write set, and repository boundary.
Do not add product behavior, dependencies, external tooling, or credentials
without explicit authorization. Pushing your branch, opening its PR, and
merging it once checks are green is your job, not an authorization step;
never commit on local `main` and never force-push. Preserve unrelated work,
run the assigned focused checks, and report the exact changed paths and results
to the wave Explorer.

Publication authority lists (`AZ_REQUIREMENTS_APPROVED_LIBRARY_PUBLICATIONS`
and `AZ_REQUIREMENTS_LIBRARY_DEPENDENCY_EXCEPTIONS`) are operator-trusted launch
configuration: whoever controls the host's launch environment is the owner.
Exception `actor` and `actor_kind` are recorded attribution, not human or owner
identity verification. Exact project, specification, dependency address,
revision/digest and project publication revision/digest bindings remain required;
exceptions waive only unpublished authority and are recorded with publication.

## Code design: speed and parallelism

Design and implement code for speed. Prefer the design that runs faster:
avoid serial round-trips, repeated engine starts and redundant rebuilds, and
measure a hot path before and after changing it. When operations in the code
are independent (engine or API calls, file reads, tests, gate lanes), run them
concurrently. Serialize only where a shared resource or ordering requires it,
such as the store allocator, a lease or the shared host, and name that resource
in the code. Speed never overrides correctness or security controls.

## Merge gate

The merge requirement is `bash tools/ci/local-gates.sh` exiting 0 on the PR's
exact head commit, with clean tracked files. This repository has no hosted PR
checks; "no checks reported" is not a pass. The script runs every gate
(`--list` prints them) and reports PASS, FAIL, SKIP or N/A per gate. When a
payload path changed since the merge-base with `origin/main`, it also builds
the `.deb`, verifies it and runs the real-engine store lifecycle test; it
runs `py-model-check` only when a model-checker path changed, and `ui-test`
(`pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm test` over an offline, frozen-lockfile restore)
only when a UI or wire-contract path changed (`--full` forces all of those).
The Modeling/Filter cross-check reads the exact AZEdge commit in
`tests/model_check/azedge.commit`, materialized from Git objects into a temporary
read-only snapshot and removed after the check. Bump that full commit SHA
deliberately when changing the pinned pair. Sibling branch and dirty files do
not affect it. A sibling `azedge` repository is discovered automatically from
the repository or worktrees parent; `AZEDGE_ROOT` optionally locates another
Git object database, never selects working-tree content. No hand-set override
is needed for the merge queue. Missing repository, pin, commit or pinned input
fails visibly as NOT-RUN, preserving the cross-check coverage.
Gates run in three concurrent lanes, with every cargo step and
engine- or port-starting suite in one sequential lane; `--self-test` checks
the path filters and lane aggregation. On an all-PASS run it writes a receipt
for the head SHA; `--fast` skips slow gates and never writes one.

`hooks/merge-gate-guard.py`, registered as a PreToolUse shell hook for Claude
Code, Codex and Cursor, refuses `gh pr merge` (and `gh api .../pulls/N/merge`)
for this repository unless that receipt exists for the PR head. The guard also
refuses a direct merge while `tools/ci/merge-queue.sh` holds its landing lock
for a different PR/head. It probes live lock ownership: a record without a
lock owner (dead pid, or a live pid that never owned the lock) does not
block, while a held lock without a published holder still blocks.

`bash tools/ci/merge-queue.sh land <PR-number>` is the sanctioned landing path.
It serializes every landing on this machine with an exclusive lock in the
receipts directory (waiting rather than failing), then fetches, merges the PR
head with the current `origin/main` in a disposable worktree, bumps `Version`
there when the landing head touches a payload path, runs the full gate on it,
pushes it to the PR branch, polls mergeability every three seconds for at most
60 checks while holding the lock (refusing a moved head), and merges it with
`gh pr merge --merge --match-head-commit`. After the gate the queue requires
a PASS receipt for the exact landing head before push/merge; a gate exit 0
alone never authorizes a merge. `MERGE_QUEUE_GATE_CMD`,
`AZ_GATE_RECEIPTS` and `MERGE_QUEUE_GH_REPO` overrides are test-only: they take effect solely against
local test origins and are refused otherwise, so production always uses the
fixed real repository, the default receipts directory (one machine-wide lock) and the full gate.
Test-origin runs address only the fixture repository named by required `MERGE_QUEUE_GH_REPO`, never the real one.
Production landing worktrees are created under the repository's visible
worktrees root, never `/tmp`. The Version bump is idempotent, so a retry
after push-success/merge-failure reuses the pushed bump. PR branches no
longer need their own Version bump; `version-guard` still runs on the landing
head, so one Version still names one payload. The only escape is the
owner launching the harness with `AZ_MERGE_GATE_OVERRIDE=<reason>`; it is
logged, never silent, and agents must not set it.

## Closeout

Before reporting completion, integrate task changes into the agreed target
through a reviewed and gated PR merge you perform yourself, verify the remote
result, and
remove task-specific worktrees and branches only after clean merged proof and
resolving live references; check `alphazede-requirements-system checkout list`
before removing a worktree. If authority, CI, conflicts, or runtime blocks this,
report the work incomplete with the exact blocker and retained paths. Never
silently leave work unmerged, discard dirty files, bypass gates, or remove a
worktree still referenced by a live process.


## Visual review of UI changes

Workspace cross-repo rule #16 applies here. Any change that alters rendered
UI (the `ui/` app and the workbench) is reviewed in a running instance before
merge, not only as a diff. Use a local dev instance, never the shared
packaged host.

- Run the candidate on a local dev or test host, never production.
- Drive every changed flow with browser automation or computer use: click
  through each changed screen and state, at desktop and phone widths.
- Capture a screenshot of each state and keep it with the review evidence.
  Never capture customer data.
- Judge against the spec, the design references, or the approved mockups:
  layout, spacing, typography, empty and error states, console errors, and
  accessibility basics.
- Visual findings join the same review-and-repair round as code findings. A
  UI that cannot be run is a typed gap with its exact error, never a pass.
- Whoever dispatches the reviewer writes the run command and the flows to
  check into the packet.

## Filing product bottlenecks

An agent that hits a repeatable bottleneck in this repository's product or
tooling files a GitHub enhancement issue here. Examples: a gate that forces
reruns, a hot file that every change conflicts on, a missing command, a manual
multi-step procedure. File through `Alphazedehq/hooks/issue-labels.py` with
kind `enhancement` (dry-run first, then `--submit`). Search for a duplicate
first and add evidence to an existing issue instead of filing a second. Use
the standard sections (Origin, Harness / version, What, Current evidence,
Expected, Acceptance), with the measured cost as evidence. File only what you
observed, never speculation. Canonical: workspace `AGENTS.md` cross-repo rule
#18.

## Fixable review findings

Never pass or accept `ACCEPT_WITH_FINDINGS` while a fixable bug remains. If an actionable review finding can be repaired without causing a regression or violating the approved contract, the verdict is `REPAIR_REQUIRED`; fix it in the authorized repair round and rerun deterministic verification. `ACCEPT_WITH_FINDINGS` is reserved for owner-approved residual risk or a finding that cannot be repaired within the approved contract without causing a regression.

Review and repair run as one pair: one independent review, then one repair
round that fixes every finding it raised. After that repair, deterministic
verification decides and the change lands; do not start another review round
unless the owner asks or the repair changed the approved scope. Never file a
review finding as an issue instead of fixing it in that repair round.


## Defect filing

Defects and gaps found during work are disposed of under COE-ELSD-008,
COE-ELSD-009 and COE-ELSD-010 of the COE-ELSD library, published REV-3
(commit 8d39a72e70b55f72fc206ef776d279b06369192c, sha256:645ada3c5044b8e2a942757e61b172e61fb28cd5aa4b8f38b3ba88aa5895940e), in alphazede/Alphazedehq
`docs/coe/engineering-lifecycle-secure-delivery/engineering-lifecycle-secure-delivery.sdoc`.

